/*****************************************************************************
    jitIterTwice -- TWO SEQUENTIAL RE-TARGETED ITERATES ON ONE VARIABLE.

    ⚠ THIS FIXTURE EXISTS BECAUSE AN EARLIER ONE DID NOT DISCRIMINATE, and that
    failure is worth more than the fixture. The first attempt fired one jitted
    iterate three times and asserted 3/3/3 -- which passed IDENTICALLY with the
    new emitted setup and with it removed, because an exhausted iterator
    restarts from firstInList, so a second fire walks the same list again
    whether or not anything re-established it. Green, and evidence of nothing.
    "An oracle is only evidence over the conditions it was captured under."

    THE DISCRIMINATING SHAPE IS displayForm's OWN: ONE variable, TWO iterates,
    RE-TARGETED between them --

        iterate g attributes on trunk;  while ++g  countA++;
        iterate g members on trunk;     while ++g  countB++;

    At EMIT time both setups run, in order, so `g` ends bound to the MEMBER
    list. If the setup is not emitted, the compiled function starts BOTH loops
    against whatever binding emit time left behind -- the members -- so the
    first loop counts members instead of attributes. If the setup IS emitted,
    each loop re-establishes its own source at run time and the two counts
    differ correctly.

    ⚠ THE TWO COUNTS MUST DIFFER, which is the injectivity rule applied to a
    construct: a trunk with equal numbers of attributes and members would give
    the same pair under both behaviours and prove nothing. Two attributes,
    three members.

    MEASURED, AND THIS IS THE RUNG'S EVIDENCE -- two points, not one:
        gate REMOVED, rebuilt:  jitted attrs=3 members=3   oracle 2/3   WRONG
        gate PRESENT, rebuilt:  jitted attrs=2 members=3   oracle 2/3   right
    The wrong answer was SILENT: degrade count 0 in both runs. So nothing except
    a value assertion over a discriminating shape would have caught it.

    THE THIRD LOOP COVERS `continue`, which had no emitter at all until today.
    itMemTwo is noPrint, so the loop visits three members and counts two -- the
    same `if noPrinT; continue;` line displayForm uses. A continue that emitted
    nothing would count three.
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(generate);
include(utilities);

search reset stack Grokking;
search Generating UnitTests bcOPs Utilities list;

register(JitIterTwice);
define
    itAttrOne;
    itAttrTwo;
    itTrunk     itAttrOne itAttrTwo
        itMemOne;
        itMemTwo noPrint;
        itMemThree;
    itCountA;
    itCountB;
    itCountC;
    itWalk code={
        itCountA = 0;
        itCountB = 0;
        iterate g attributes on itTrunk;
        while ++g
            ++itCountA;
        iterate g members on itTrunk;
        while ++g
            ++itCountB;
        itCountC = 0;
        iterate g members on itTrunk;
        while ++g;
            if noPrinT; continue;
            ++itCountC;
        };
    ;

itCountA = 0; itCountB = 0; itCountC = 0;
testing(itWalk);
print "IT fire 1 : attrs =" itCountA " members =" itCountB " kept =" itCountC " (want 2 3 2)":;

itCountA = 0; itCountB = 0; itCountC = 0;
jitRefire();
print "IT fire 2 : attrs =" itCountA " members =" itCountB " kept =" itCountC " (want 2 3 2)":;

itCountA = 0; itCountB = 0; itCountC = 0;
itWalk();
print "IT interpreted : attrs =" itCountA " members =" itCountB " kept =" itCountC " (oracle -- want 2 3 2)":;

print "IT SENTINEL -- run reached the end":;
stop();
